.bp
.in 0
.ds
.ig
.ul 1
..
4.0 OPERATION

.ti 3
This section contains what is hoped to be a concise guide to
operating a batch station under the UNIX Level 6 system.

.ig
.ul 1
..
4.1 POWER UP

.ti 3
all is quiet, nothing is stirring not even a fan.
.ti 3
take the PDP 11/40 front panel key from its hiding place
and insert it in the lock.
in the following order do:

.in 10
 1) Put ENABLE/HALT switch to HALT.
 2) Turn the key to POWER.
 3) Power on all peripherals not yet on.
 4) Place the discs on the correct drives
	* system disc on drive 0
	* spool disc on drive 1
	* others on 2,3,4.....
		(this configuration may actually vary on some systems)
 5) Put RUN/LOAD switch to RUN on all drives
    with discs in place.
 6) Boot. (ref section 4.2)
 7) Turn key to LOCK.

.in 0
.ti 3
you may now return the key to its hiding place, walk to the console
and complete the startup dialogue.  (ref section 4.3.1)

.ig
.ul 1
..
4.2 BOOTING

.ti 3
when powered up the 11/40 has only one program in its memory.
this program is called the bootstrap program.
it is stored in an unerasable "read only memory" (ROM)
and when executed will load another larger program and cause it to execute.
.ti 3
the execution of the ROM bootstrap to load the UNIX bootstrap (UBOOT)
into core and execute it is called bootstrapping or "booting".
UBOOT then loads the UNIX system and starts it running.
.ti 3
Among the disc batch stations on campus there are three different bootstrap
programs in use.
.ti 3
Type one is in use at Mathematics and
Commerce.
Execution is initiated as follows.

.in 10
 1) Ensure the front panel lock is at POWER position.
 2) Ensure ENABLE/HALT switch is at HALT.
 3) Set switch register to 773100 octal.
	i.e.	uuuuuuduuddudddddd	u=switch up
 4) Depress and release the LOAD ADDRESS switch.
 5) Set switch register to 777406 octal.
	i.e.	uuuuuuuuuuddddduud	d=switch down
 6) Put ENABLE/HALT switch to ENABLE.
 7) Depress and release the START switch.

.in 0
.ti 3
Type two is in use at the Library and Computing Services Unit.
execution is initiated as follows.

.in 10
 1) Ensure the front panel lock is at POWER position.
 2) Ensure ENABLE/HALT switch is at halt.
 3) Set switch register to 773110 octal.
	i.e.	uuuuuuduuddudduddd
 4) Depress and release LOAD ADDRESS switch.
 5) Put ENABLE/HALT switch to ENABLE.
 6) Depress and release the START switch.

.in 0
.ti 3
Type three is in use at the Electrical Engineering and Mechanical Engineering.
Execution is initiated as follows.

.in 10
 1) Ensure the front panel lock is at POWER position.
 2) Ensure ENABLE/HALT switch is at halt.
 3) Set switch register to 773100 octal.
	i.e.	uuuuuuduuddudddddd
 4) Depress and release LOAD ADDRESS switch.
 5) Put ENABLE/HALT switch to ENABLE.
 6) Depress and release the START switch.


.in 0
.ti 3
Which ever way you initiate bootstrap execution,
once you have booted you need only sit down and complete the startup dialogue.

.ig
.ul 1
..
4.3 RUNNING

.ti 3
All console entries are terminated by the CR (RETURN)
key.

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
4.3.1 Startup

.ti 10
Having booted the system, the console prompts you to
complete the startup dialogue which at present
requires only one answer to a question like
.nf
.ss
.ne 6
.in 20

			Wed Jul  7 09:31:07 1976
if acceptable just <cr>
otherwise enter date in the form
mmddhhmm[yy]

where the mmddhhmm[yy] is requesting the month number,
day in moth number, hour in day number (24 hrs),
minute in hour number and an optional year number (eg 75).
.in 7
.ds
.fi

.ti 10
The startup procedure also performs a file system
storage consistency check (ICHECK) and a file system
directory consistency check (DCHECK) on all
usually mounted discs.
Checks required on any other mounted discs must be specifically
initiated by the operator.
.ti 10
The output from these checks is displayed on the console
and should be examined for any error indications (ref section 4.3.3).
A typical out appears below.

.ss
.ne 33
		/dev/rrk0:
		freei     98
		spcl      40
		files    324
		large    114
		direc     24
		indir    114
		used    3619
		free     294
		high    3999
		/dev/rrk1:
		freei    104
		spcl       0
		files    680
		large    127
		direc     36
		indir    128
		used    3988
		free     797
		high    4871
		/dev/rrk2:
		freei    205
		spcl       0
		files    555
		large    152
		direc     48
		indir    152
		used    4050
		free     735
		high	4073
		/dev/rrk0:
		/dev/rrk1:
		/dev/rrk2:
.ds

.ig
.ul 1
..
4.3.2 Login

.ti 10
After an end-of-file (CTRL/D) or boot the terminal will eventually ask you to
"LOGIN:".
In response to this you type in (enter) a login name.
for the batch station system this is "OPER".
.ti 10
If OPER has been password protected the console asks for the
"PASSWORD:" to be entered to complete login.
The distributed batch system has no OPER password.
.ti 10
When login has been completed correctly UNIX
prompts with a "%".
character.
You may now enter commands.

.ig
.ul 1
..
4.3.3 Commands

.ti 10
the following list of commands should be read in conjunction with
section 4.3.4 in order to correlate the commands with any
troubles although trouble is rare.
.ti 10
we shall present the commands and their descriptions in the
order you will most probably use them.
.ti 10
Parameters enclosed in square brackets ([]) are optional and options
may be nested where indicated.

.in 16
.ti 7
ICHECK - this is used to establish file system storage consistency.
	usage:
		ICHECK [flsys1 flsys2 ... flsysn]
	where:
		flsys - file system to check.
			e.g. "/DEV/RK1" or "/DEV/RRK1"

.ti 16
Normal ICHECK output includes a report on

		* The number of free inodes,
		* The number of special files,
		* The total number of files,
		* The number of large and huge files,
		* The number of directories,
		* The number of indirect blocks, and the number of
		  double-indirect blocks in huge files,
		* The number of blocks used in files,
		* The number of free blocks,
		* The number of the highest block allocated.

.ti 16
Using the raw mode device file (RRKn) will speed execution
of this command since the disc is read in large chunks.
.ti 16
A similar output from "ICHECK" to that shown below is satisfactory.

.ss
.ne 30
		/dev/rrk0:
		freei    105
		spcl      40
		files    324
		large    114
		direc     24
		indir    114
		used    3618
		free     295
		high    3999
		/dev/rrk1:
		freei    227
		spcl       0
		files    678
		large    126
		direc     36
		indir    127
		used    3963
		free     822
		high    4523
		/dev/rrk2:
		freei     99
		spcl       0
		files    610
		large    163
		direc     46
		indir    163
		used    4228
		free     557
		high    3756
.ds

.ti 16
however if any other lines appear between the "/dev/..." lines and
the following "FREEI......." lines
you should IMMEDIATELY consult a guru
(Appendix G).
.ti 16
though this is rare it must not be taken lightly and no unskilled
attempt should be made to correct the error or use the system until
corrected.
.ti 16
the output from an "ICHECK" of a bad disc appears
below.

.ss
.ne 13
		/dev/rrk1:
		2 dups in free
		3 missing
		unallocated and non-zero inode: 293
		freei     99
		spcl       0
		files    195
		large     34
		direc     29
		indir     34
		used    1043
		free    3765
		high    2675

.ds

.ti 7
DCHECK - This command is used to check file system directory consistency.
	usage:
		DCHECK flsys
	where:
		flsys - file system to check

.ti 16
Raw mode device files will speed execution of this command.
.ti 16
Normal DCHECK output consists of only one line giving the device file
name.

		/dev/rrk0:

Any error lines appear immediately after this line for example

.ss
.ne 14
		/dev/rrk2:
		entries	link cnt
		323	0	0
		327	0	0
		462	0	0
		624	0	0
		632	0	0
		640	0	0
		662	0	0
		666	0	0
		676	0	0
		684	0	0
		710	0	0
		722	0	0
.ds
.ti 16
The errors if any should not be taken lightly.
Consult a GURU.

.ti 7
DATE - In order to maintain the statistics (and other things) UNIX
must have a reasonably accurate idea of the date and time.
this command is used to manipulate the date and time from a terminal.
	usage:
		DATE [mmddhhmm[yy]]
	where:
		mm - 2 digit month of the year
		dd - 2 digit day of the month
		hh - 2 digit hour of the day
		mm - 2 digit minute of the hour
		yy - optional 2 digit year number
			(eg 75)

.ti 16
Once the date has been set in the startup dialogue only the "Super User"
may reset the date.
.ti 16
DATE with no parameters will display the present UNIX date
and time.

.ti 7
BATCH - This command starts execution of the Batch System processes.
.ti 16
This should be the only command necessary to start complete
communication with the CYBER , start
reading cards, start printing printout and every other little thing.
	usage:
		BATCH [options ...]

.ti 16
Various options are available when Batch is started.  Only the first two
characters need be given.
They are:

	cold		- do a cold start
	tlnnn		- set max time limit to 'nnn' seconds
	clnnn		- set card limit to 'nnn' cards
	jlnnn		- set cyber job limit to 'nnn' jobs
	rlnnn		- set limit on sectors received to 'nnn'
	slnnn		- set limit on lines in submit files to 'nnn'
	cyber		- don't allow cyber jobs to enter system
	local		- don't allow local ...
	submit		- don't allow submit jobs ...
	nc		- don't use card reader
	np		- don't use line printer
	macro		- don't allow macro jobs
	fortran		- don't allow fortran jobs
	shell		- don't allow shell jobs

BATCH checks the queue status available from the last time BATCH
was running and if all queues were empty performs a COLD start (see below).
.ti 16
If the queues were not empty, BATCH provides you with a DQ display.
If the queues are in a mess, the display will not appear (since "DQ"
crashed) and instead you will get the message "Queues are screwed".
In any case, it then asks the question

	"re-initialize the batch system ? "

.ti 16
To this you may reply either <return> if you wish to
retain information present in the Batch System
(tables of jobs, where they are etc) or "COLD" if you wish to start afresh
with no knowledge retained from an earlier time.
.ti 16
The "COLD" reply will obtain the response

	"directory being cleared & queues reinitialized"

.ti 16
The carriage return response will produce a summary of what the Batch System
thought was happening when it was last closed down, similar to the following.

	"qeueues being restored"
	"jobname - was being read/received"
	"jobname - was being transmitted"
	"jobname - was spooling"
	"jobname - was being received"
	"jobname - was executing"
	"jobname - was unidentified"

where jobname is some batch job.
.ti 16
Somewhere in the above dialogue, occasionally a CYBER "PLEASE LOGIN."
message may appear but never fear, you may ignore it.
BATCH will login to the CYBER using the correct individual Batch Station
account and password.

.ti 7
local - this is the same as "batch" (and takes the
same options) but does not start the cyber daemons "SPL", "JTD" and "EAR".

.ti 7
L - This is the command used to login to the CYBER
but its use is hardly ever required because the entire login sequence
is completed for you by the BATCH process.
.ti 16
"L" may be needed if the Batch Station is logged off using the "B" command
without closing down the whole Batch System.
	usage:
		L account [passwd]
	where:
		account - CYBER batch acc. no.
		passwd - is the account password,

.ti 7
PS - This command is used to list information on the
processes currently running on the 11/40.
	usage:
		PS [y]
	where:
		y - may be a number of different parameters
.ti 16
The most common parameters given to "PS" are "A" and "X" as below.
		"PS AX"
.ti 16
To aid explanation, here is an output from this command.
.ss
.ne 33

	TTY  PID   TIME  COMMAND
	?:     0  93.30s unix
	?:     1   1.58s init
	     238   1.38s -
	a:    35   0.16s - d
	      14  23.58s update
	      18   0.04s errlog
	      20   0.50s cron
	      22 103m41s .sponge
	b:    36   0.16s - d
	c:    37   0.16s - d
	e:    38   0.14s - t
	f:    39   0.20s - t
	g:    40   0.18s - t
	h:    41   0.16s - t
	k:    42   0.16s - k
	l:   214   0.22s - T
	o:    44  10.44s -
	o:   148   0.82s /bin/sh /usr/bin/s
	o:   149   0.22s sleep 50000
	     240   3.34s ps ax
	     221   0.26s .print
	     220   0.48s * batch *
	     222   1.72s .jtd
	     223   0.16s .spl
	     224   0.22s .ear
	     225   0.12s .crd
	     226   0.18s .mac
	     227   0.14s .ftn
	     228   0.10s .list

.ds
.ti 16
All process ID numbers (PIDs) are displayed and for processes
not swapped out onto disc (at the time the "PS AX" was done)
a fairly close description of the process is displayed
beside the PID.
.ti 16
The TTY column gives the tty id-number of the terminal which initiated
the process, processes without such a number are those
started from the terminal which initiated the PS command itself.
.ti 16
From this you are able
see that all batch station processes are running (the whole gaggle of PIDs
from 220 onwards owned by tty8 in this case).

.ti 7
DQ - This is a command used to interrogate the local tables
of spooled card decks, jobs at the CYBER, local jobs and 
spooled print jobs.
a lot of other information is also printed relating to the status of
the batch system, which is pretty self-explanatory.
any messages that the central site operator sends out
are displayed as well.
Jobs flagged with '+' are in the alternate print queue at cyber.
jobs flagged with '-' will not be printed on return from the cyber,
but rather will remain in the batch working directory.
	usage:
		DQ

.ti 7
AUTOQ - This command invokes the DQ command with some extra information.
AUTOQ will continuously execute a DQ command every 30 seconds until
stopped with the DELETE or RUBOUT keys.
	usage:
		AUTOQ

.ti 7
LPR - This command is used to spool
locally generated files to be printed.
it is very handy for producing banner pages to line up the printer
after a paper jam or change.
	usage:
		LPR [file1 file2 ... filen]
	where:
		file - is a file you wish printed
.ti 16
if invoked with no parameters it will spool all further entries
from the console until an end of file (CTRL/D) and then
print all spooled entries preceded by a banner page.
therefore the following will produce a banner page only

	"LPR"
	(CTRL/D)

.ti 7
PASSWD - This command is used to change or create a password
associated with a specific UNIX login name.
	usage:
		PASSWD lname [passwd]
	where:
		lname - is the login name
		passwd - is the new password for lname

if the password is not given with the command,
it will prompt you for it (with echo turned off).
.ti 16
for example if you wished to change the OPER login name password
to "magnexcellent" you would enter

	"PASSWD OPER"
	Password:  MAGNEXCELLENT (doesn't get echoed)

.ti 16
Two double quotes ("") is a null (removed) passwd.

.ti 7
A - Rewind the printfile presently being received from the CYBER.
This printfile is never the one presently
printing on the printer.
Consult the DQ display to find the job being received from the CYBER.
	usage:
		A

.ti 7
B - This command
logs off from the CYBER but does NOT close down the
whole Batch System.
Local processing may continue until a STOP command is given.
The "L" command will allow you to log back into the CYBER.
The log off is ultimately verified by the appearance of the
message
	"KRONOS 2.1.2 LEVEL 439."
.ti 16
If this is not forthcoming then the CYBER may be dead.
	usage:
		B

.ti 7
C - Asks the CYBER to send the rest of a previously suspended
printout to the batch station.
	usage:
		C

.ti 7
D - This command purges a job in the CYBER
output queue.
	usage:
		D name O
	where:
		name - jobname from a "Q O" command
			or deduced from a "DQ" display.

.ti 7
D - This command will purge a job from any CYBER
queue other than the output queue.
	usage:
		D name
	where:
		name - jobname from a "Q x" command
			or deduced from a DQ display.
			(x is I,E or R)

.ti 7
E LP - This command will cancel the CYBER batch job currently being received
from the CYBER.
Use the DQ display to find the job currently being received.
	usage:
		E LP

.ti 7
E CR - This command will cancel the CYBER batch job currently
being sent to the CYBER.
Use the DQ display to find the job being sent.
	usage:
		E CR

.ti 7
CANCEL CR - This command cancels the job at present on the
card reader.
	usage:
		CANCEL CR

.ti 7
CANCEL LP - This command cancels the job printing on the printer.
	usage:
		CANCEL LP

.ti 7
CANCEL macro - this command will kill any job produced by
the macro process.
	usage:
		CANCEL macro

.ti 7
CANCEL fortran (or ftn) - as for macro

.ti 7
CANCEL shell - as for macro

.ti 7
PRSTOP - This command will delete the printout currently on the printer,
and is the same as "cancel lp".
	usage:
		PRSTOP

.ti 7
RESTART - This command causes the printout at present on the printer
to be restarted immediately, from the beginning, complete
with banner page.
At batch stations where the printer has a habit of of tearing
the paper this is a very useful command.
	usage:
		restart

.ti 7
repeat - this will cause the job currently printing to be printed
all over again when is is finished.
	usage:
		repeat

.ti 7
F id - This command will make EXPORT send back printouts with a queue id
of "id".  The normal id is 0, and the "alternate queue" is 1.
Therefore a job placed in the alternate print queue
by the "o" command can be retrieved by "f 1".
	usage:
		f 1

.ti 7
F - This command will cause you to revert
to printing normal files after messing around with the "F id" command.
	usage:
		F

.ti 7
G - this command asks the CYBER to accept more cards.
The use of this command is unnecessary under
the UNIX batch system
as the 200-UT driver issues a "G" command every 20 seconds.
However it will cause no harm to use it.
	usage:
		G

.ti 7
I - This command is a general reset
of any interrupted CYBER operations (what ever that means ??).
The use of this command is unnecessary under the UNIX
batch system.
	usage:
		I

.ti 7
M - This command displays a message on the CYBER
operators "B" display.
	usage:
		M message
	where:
		message - max 18 character message, may be in quotes

.ti 7
N - This command instructs the CYBER to stop sending
jobs to the batch station
at the end of a job.
	usage:
		N

.ti 7
n c - revert to receiving again.
this will cancel the effect of an 'n' command.
	usage:
		n c

.ti 7
O - This command will place a job currently in the Cyber output queue
into some other output queue e.g. for another batch station.
It may also place the job into the "alternate" queue, where it will stay
until released by the "F 1" command.  Jobs in the alternate queue
are flagged by "+".
	usage:
		O jobname XXXX
	where XXXX is one of:
		ELEC,
		MECH,
		MATH,
		COMM,
		CHEM,
		TERC,
		CIVL,
		APSC,
		PHYS,
		CENT	(central site),
		ALT	(alternate queue).

.ti 7
P - This command is used to alter the priority of a job
in the CYBER output queue.
	usage:
		P name p
	where:
		name - jobname from "Q O" command
			or deduced from a DQ display.
		p - 2 digit priority
.ti 16
the 2 digit priority given replaces the 2 most significant digits
of the 4 digit priority number associated with the jobname
obtained from a "Q O" command.

.ti 7
Q B - This command displays jobs to be
printed at the central site.
	usage:
		Q B

.ti 7
Q I - This command displays jobs from your batch station
currently in the CYBER input queue.
	usage:
		Q I

.ti 7
Q O - This command displays jobs from your batch station
currently in the CYBER output queue.
	usage:
		Q O

.ti 7
Q R - This command displays jobs from your batch station currently
in the CYBER rollout queue.
	usage:
		Q R

.ti 7
Q E - This command displays jobs from your batch station
currently at a CYBER control point (in the active queue).
	usage:
		Q E

.ti 7
Q N - This command displayes the CYBER name of the
last job read from your batch station.
	usage:
		Q N

.ti 7
Q I A - Tells you the total number of jobs in the
CYBER input queue.
	usage:
		Q I A

.ti 7
Q O A - Tells you the total number of jobs in
the CYBER output queue.
	usage:
		Q O A

.ti 7
Q R A - Tells you the total number of jobs in
the CYBER rollout queue.
	usage:
		Q R A

.ti 7
R - This command asks the CYBER to accept more cards.
The use of this command is unnecessary under the UNIX batch
system.
	usage:
		R

.ti 7
S - Suspend printfile presently being received from the CYBER
and note the position in the file for later restart of transmission.
This printfile is never the one presently printing on the printer.
Consult the DQ display to find the job being received from the CYBER.
	usage:
		S

.ti 7
SUBMIT - This is a terminal command used to place the contents of
a UNIX file into the Batch System input queue.
.ti 16
The command will only work when the Batch System is running.
.ti 16
The file must contain all relevant control cards and EORs (CTRL/R)
needed for normal batch processing.
The UNIX end-of-file condition implies the EOI.
.ti 16
Because batch processing is very card oriented,
lines over 80 columns long are flagged and cause the job to be immediately
flushed to the local printer.
.ti 16
Once submitted the job proceeds as if it had come in via the card reader,
although it is flagged internally as having come from a terminal.
.ti 16
if a flag '-' is given then the job is not printed on return from
the cyber, but rather will be returned to the user's directory under
the encoded batch file name.
The user is notified by mail when this happens.
If the file cannot be returned, it will be printed instead.
Note that the file is returned exactly as received from the Cyber
i.e. it will have the banner page, dayfile & blank compression
in it.
.ti 16
this is useful for intercepting certain listings from the cyber.
	usage:
		SUBMIT [-] filename
	where:
		filename - name of UNIX file to be submitted

.ti 16
SUBMIT, like the CRD process, checks CYBER jobs for invalid
job cards and, if found
does not send the job to the CYBER.
Instead the job is flushed straight to the local printer with an error message
of the form

	"####  illegal job card"

followed by some error message.

.ti 7
closedown - This command is used to close down the UNIX system at the
end of a days confusion.
closedown causes the UNIX systems idea of the date and time of day to be written to disc,
does a SYNC and
checks to see if there are any other UNIX users signed on
and warns you of this fact with a message.

.ti 7
U - Returns printfile presently being received
from the CYBER to the CYBER output queue at zero priority.
This printfile is never the one presently printing on the printer.
Consult the DQ display to find the job currently being received from the CYBER.
	usage:
		U

.ti 7
WHO - This command tells you who is currently
logged on to the UNIX system.
	usage:
		WHO
.ti 16
The information displayed includes the users login name, their
teletype number and the time of logging in.
.ti 16
Unless you have other terminals connected to your 11/40 the result
of this command will always be similar to
.ss
.ne 1

	oper    tty8 Jul  7 13:42

.ds
.ti 16
If you have other terminals connected then it may be something like

.ss
.ne 3

	oper    tty8 Jul  7 13:42
	ianj    ttyg Jul  7 12:44
	pete    ttyk Jul  7 13:51

.ds
.ti 16
People become very irate if their 11/40 drops out from under them
while they are using a terminal so you should always check WHO
else is logged on before you close the system down.
The CLOSEDOWN command performs this check this for you.

.ti 7
STOP - This command closes down all Batch System
processes.
The B command should be used to log off from the CYBER before
the STOP command is given, if only for aesthetic reasons.
.ti 16
Batch System close down is verified
by the appearence of the message

	"** batch has completed"
.ti 16
If the Batch System is already closed you are told

	"batch not active"


	usage:
		STOP

.ti 7
JA - This command starts the JA process.
JA cannot be started while the Batch System is running and
so its first message
asks you to verify that BATCH is closed
to which you reply "yes" or ""no".
.ti 16
JA analyses and condenses the bulk statistics
compiled by the Batch System into a summary file for later use.
JA will produce a printout with all sorts of nice information.
Try one soon.
	usage:
		JA

.ti 7
SYNC - 
UNIX maintains a set
of tables in memory by which it accesses the disc file system.
These tables in part constitute what is known as the
UNIX Super Block.
Periodically, every 30 seconds if the file system is active, the Super Block is written to disc
so as to preserve a reasonably up to date copy (for the next system
BOOT) in case anything catastrophic happens in the next
30 seconds.
If the system status has not changed in the last 30 seconds
the needless update is not performed.
.ti 16
Obviously the disc copy of the SuperBlock
may only be a rough estimate of what the file system was like since
a great number of files can be modified in 30 seconds.
However, barring catastrophes, UNIX requires an accurate copy of the
Super Block and this is where the SYNC command comes in.
.ti 16
SYNC does nothing else but immediately write the current
copy of the Super Block to disc.
the command is usually invoked when the system is in a fairly static
state (most processes to do with the batch station and local terminals
stopped) and so there is little chance of any new files being created
before you can halt the CPU or close the system down.
.ti 16
The CLOSEDOWN command performs additional checks and should be used in preference
to SYNC at system close down.

	usage:
		SYNC

.ti 7
COUNT - show the number of line errors.
This will give a display of the number of each type of line error.
Any abnormal number (I don't quite know what is normal) should be reported.
	Usage:
		count [unixfile]
	where:
		unixfile is the file for the symbol table (default /unix)

.ti 7
DISABLE - Disable a batch process.
This command will allow the operator to disable a batch
process such as FORTRAN etc.  Any attempt to make use of that process
will be flagged with an error message.
	Usage:
		disable process ...
	where process is one of:
		all		(disable the lot),
		cyber		(no cyber jobs allowed),
		submit		(no submit jobs),
		local		(no local jobs)
		shell
		fortran, ftn
		macro.

Only the first two characters need be given

.ti 7
ENABLE - Enable a batch process.
This is the inverse of the "DISABLE" command, and usage is similar.
	Usage:
		enable proc ...

.ti 7
ENTL - Enter maximum time limit.
Local jobs are assigned a default time limit of 20 seconds.
By specifying a parameter on the job card, one may increase this limit
up to a certain limit.
The default limit is 120 seconds, but may be set by this command.
	Usage:
		entl nnn
	where:
		nnn is the max time limit allowed (< 65536 seconds).

.ti 7
ENCL - Enter card limit.
Cyber jobs reading on the card reader have a default limit of 1000 cards.
Any more than this will cause the job to be flushed.
This limit can be changed by this command.
	Usage:
		encl nnn
	where:
		nnn is the no. cards allowed (< 65536).

.ti 7
ENRL - Enter receive limit.
A limit of 100 sectors is placed on jobs received from Cyber.
Any more than this and the job is automatically placed in the "alternate
print" queue and a message is given.
Note that the number of sectors displayed by "q o" is NOT the
number of sectors received, since buffers are reformatted before
being sent down.
It is, however, a rough guide.
This limit may be changed by this command.
	Usage:
		enrl nnn
	where
		nnn is the number of sectors allowed (< 65536).

.ti 7
ENJL - Enter job limit.
There is a maximum of 75 Cyber jobs allowed in the Batch queues at once.
Any more than this entered via "submit" or card reader will cause the job to
be flushed.
The limit may be set by this command.
	Usage:
		enjl nnn
	where:
		nnn is the number of cyber jobs allowed (<= 100).
.ti 16
Note that "enjl 0" will disable all cyber job entry, and that "nnn" can
only go up to 100 (total number of queue elements).

.ti 7
ENSL - Enter submit limit.
This is basically the same as "encl" except it applies to the number of
lines in cyber "submit" jobs.
	Usage:
		ensl nnn

.ti 7
PURGE - Purge jobs from Batch queues.
This command allows one to remove jobs from Batch queues, or to delete
an entire queue.
It may be used in three ways.

1)	Usage:
		purge Q
	where:
		Q is the queue (see below) will cause a question to
be asked
.ti 16
"Purge of entire queue? Confirm:".
.ti 16
Any entry starting with "y" will cause deletion of the entire queue,
otherwise the command exits.
.sp
2)	Usage:
		purge Q -
.ti 16
will cause the program to step through the given queue printing job names.
To delete an entry, type "y".
To step to the next entry, just type carriage return.
To exit immediately, type "x".
Any other entry causes the line to be repeated.
It works just like DSW(I) in the UNIX Programmers Manual.
.sp
3)	Usage:
		purge Q job ...
	where:
		job is a job in that queue will cause deletion
of that job.
.ti 16
A job name may be preceded by "-n" in which case the "n'th" occurrence
of that job gets deleted.  Note that "purge Q -2 fred -3 fred" does not
do what you may think it does.  It will not delete the third job, but the
fourth since one preceding job has already been purged, and the queue
is rescanned each time.  A slight aberration.
.ti 16
.sp
"Q" may be one of:
	a	at cyber
	c	for cyber
	f	fortran
	l	list
	m	macro
	p	print
	s	shell

.ti 7
STATUS - Give status of Batch system.
This command merely prints out some interesting (?) info about the
current status of the environment of Batch.
It is not really useful unless there is something odd going on...
	Usage:
		status

.ti 7
QLOOK - Look at the queues in the checkpoint file.
This command merely decodes the information in the checkpoint file
in a human-readable form.
It is not really useful unless there is something odd going on ...
	Usage:
		qlook

.nx 6om4.3
